            <ArticleTitle>Treatment of post-electroconvulsive therapy headache with topical methyl salicylate.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e17-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/YCT.0b013e318245c640</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Headache after administration of electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is common, affecting approximately half of patients treated. Post-ECT headache is typically treated with acetaminophen or nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs but occasionally requires agents such as sumatriptan, opioids, or β-blockers. We report on a patient whose severe post-ECT headaches responded completely to methyl salicylate ointment, applied to the area of his temporalis and masseter muscles. Topical methyl salicylate is generally well tolerated and may be a viable option for some patients with post-ECT headache.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
